  "slug": "adaptive-design-curves-8294",
  "summary": "Compact learned representations from sequence and contact maps are being tested for interpretable links to regulatory and structural genome features.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is learning compact, reusable representations that connect genome sequence or structure to regulatory and organizational features. C-Fetch uses a weakly KL-regularized 3D autoencoder to retrieve reference-guided 3D chromatin contact-map features, validated on Polycomb and loop contexts. CNN-CR, an interpretable neural network, learns compact shared promoter motif families that predict expression across 116 human biosamples, and these motifs are interpreted differently by cell type. A third item, a DNA-first framework, fuses natural-vector DNA features with positional recurrence to nominate array-associated RT loci in genomes. What is new is the emphasis on compact, shared representations that are interpreted across biological contexts. The dominant thread is clearest in the first two items, while the third applies a related DNA-feature approach to a different question.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-09"
}
